#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences II continues the development of advanced mathematical techniques used throughout modern physics. Building on the analytical framework established in the first course, students study orthogonality, multivariable integration, vector calculus theorems, and advanced methods for differential equations.\nThe course emphasizes mathematical structures that arise naturally in physics, including inner product spaces, orthogonal projections, integral theorems of vector calculus, and transform methods for solving differential equations. These tools provide the mathematical foundation for analyzing electromagnetic fields, wave phenomena, dynamical systems, and statistical models encountered in advanced physics courses." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with the theory of projections in inner product spaces. Students study norms, inner products, orthogonality, and orthogonal complements in vector spaces over the real and complex numbers. Techniques such as orthogonal projections, the Gram–Schmidt process, and QR factorization allow vectors to be decomposed into orthogonal components. These ideas play a role in Fourier analysis, signal decomposition, and numerical methods used in physical simulations.\nStudents then explore quadratic forms and the diagonalization of symmetric matrices. The spectral theorem provides a geometric interpretation of symmetric transformations and leads to techniques for analyzing energy functions and stability in physical systems. Singular value decomposition is also introduced as a powerful matrix factorization with applications in numerical modeling and data analysis.\nApplications of linear algebra follow, including least-squares methods for solving overdetermined systems. These methods arise when fitting theoretical models to experimental data and when approximating solutions to physical equations.\nThe course then develops multivariable integration in three dimensions. Students compute triple integrals over general regions and apply coordinate transformations such as cylindrical and spherical coordinates. These techniques allow physicists to compute quantities such as mass distributions, charge densities, and gravitational or electric potentials.\nStudents next study line integrals and surface integrals of scalar and vector fields. Concepts such as circulation, flux, and path independence lead to the fundamental theorems of vector calculus, including Green’s theorem, the divergence theorem, and Stokes’ theorem. These results form the mathematical framework underlying conservation laws and the differential and integral formulations of electromagnetic theory.\nApplications of multivariable calculus follow, including vector mechanics and optimization. Students analyze motion using vector-valued functions, apply Newton’s second law to systems in the plane, and study work–energy relationships. Multiple integrals are used to compute mass, centers of mass, moments of inertia, and other physical quantities.\nThe course then returns to differential equations, extending earlier techniques to systems of linear differential equations. Students analyze dynamical systems using eigenvalues, phase portraits, and matrix methods, which appear in models of interacting systems such as coupled oscillators or predator–prey dynamics.\nLaplace transforms are introduced as a powerful tool for solving linear differential equations with discontinuous inputs or initial conditions. Boundary value problems and Fourier series are then developed, providing mathematical techniques used to solve differential equations describing heat flow, vibrating strings, wave propagation, and other physical phenomena.\nThe course concludes with numerical methods for differential equations and advanced probability topics. Students analyze the accuracy and stability of numerical schemes used in computational physics and study multivariate probability distributions and the central limit theorem, which appear in statistical physics and experimental analysis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1012 155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1013 "PS2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046660 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "24958065-b095-50f9-98ac-e7c4369816b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1031 "Expected Values of Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1033 730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048317 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "9438800f-fe1e-5478-b0c5-e3b869cf359e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1031 "Equivalent Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1033 553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048508 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1031 "Sampling Distributions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1033 3864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049367 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "ef34739b-f46e-5073-bcb9-1acc5022fae9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1031 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1033 2980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050606 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "57fe5849-4b30-59b2-99bd-4fb2b776d659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186115305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1031 "The Mean of a Data Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1033 1634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050862 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6c4b52b3-801d-5a43-b903-ae827a2a786c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33bedb30-2c99-52e8-a665-e3bddeed7e13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186156993 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu.$ Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $5+1 = 6$ rings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(5\\cdot 16.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(82.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\  & =16.75\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14 , \\quad 11, \\quad 7\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157172 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1039 "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157355 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186412759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1039 "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186156993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157392 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1045 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157528 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "44aff9fc-2508-51a9-baf1-3f855c1f0a4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143190" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ea5fc2c-4d2b-5104-893c-7896d0999b42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157599 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,12\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(18+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{4} \\\\  & =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1061 29343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fab3a24b-2709-54fe-985c-5fe95ec0712c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1061 29347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a21dbb27-5fb2-54ba-a76f-f7231e67b5fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1061 38136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c4fadf35-c701-59e2-9c8b-d385cd3565ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158287 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1061 38137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d85b5ed-e218-547b-81cf-36a60c612b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158288 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1061 38142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2edb1e12-aed2-5310-a098-962a8588a4f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158289 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1064 "In statistics, we often want to use sample data to deduce (or **infer**) the values of various **population parameters**. The two most common population parameters that we wish to find are\n\n- the **population mean** $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the **population variance** $\\sigma^2.$\n\nThe population mean is the mean of the *entire* population. Likewise, the population variance is the variance of the *entire* population. We should consider these values as *fixed* unknowns. \n\nSuppose, for example, we want to find the mean number of pages per book at a particular library. In other words, we wish to find the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is impractical to count the pages in every book. So, instead, we might approximate $\\mu$ using the following sampling procedure:\n\n- Randomly choose, say, $5$ books off the shelf.\n\n- Count the number of pages in each book.\n\n- Compute the mean of the $5$ observations.\n\nIn this case, the **sample size** $n=5.$\n\nSuppose we follow this procedure and get the following **observations**:\n\n$$\n201, \\qquad 506, \\qquad 58, \\qquad 104, \\qquad 99\n$$\n\nIt's convenient to denote the individual observations as $x_i.$ So, in this case, we have \n\n$$\nx_1 = 201,\\qquad x_2 = 506,\\qquad \\ldots,\\qquad x_5 = 99.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the mean $\\overline{x}$ of our sample as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(201+506+58+104+99) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 968 \\\\  & =193.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The value $\\bar{x} = 193.6$ is called an **estimate** of the population mean $\\mu.$ We sometimes write $\\hat{\\mu} = 193.6$ to denote that the value $193.6$ is an estimate of $\\mu.$\n\nIt's important to realize that our estimate $\\bar{x}$ depends entirely on this particular sample. We'd get a different estimate for $\\mu$ if we took a different sample." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1065 4738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "359539bd-254d-5083-aa76-577b8a159438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158531 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1064 "It is helpful to think of the observations of a particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1,\\quad x_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad x_n\n$$\n\nas a particular instance of data generated by a sequence of random variables \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n.\n$$\n\nLet's make sure we're clear about the notation:\n\n- $X_i$ is a *random variable* that denotes the $i$th observation in a random sample. Its value changes when we conduct a new sample.\n\n- $x_i$ is the value of the $i$th observation in a particular sample.\n\nWe typically assume that our sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ has the following properties:\n\n- They are **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**), meaning they are independent and have the same probability distribution. Moreover, they have the same distribution as the population.\n\n- Their mean is equal to the population mean:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}(X_i) = \\mu, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\n- Their variance is equal to the population variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}(X_i) = \\sigma^2, \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nWe often refer to a sequence of random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ with these properties as a **random sample of size** $\\boldsymbol{n}.$\n\nIn general, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n,$ then the **sample mean** is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i .\n$$\n\nWe say that the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an **estimator** of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, so is $\\overline{X}.$ The probability distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is called the **sampling distribution** of $\\overline{X}.$ The sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is an important topic we'll discuss in detail in a future lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1065 6297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b79ae73d-8ebe-5dda-916d-6eeb61ce850d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158560 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1064 "One important feature of $\\overline{X}$ is that it's an **unbiased estimator** of $\\mu.$ This means that the expected value of $\\overline{X}$ equals $\\mu,$ that is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can easily show that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ using the properties of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot (\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot n\\mu  \\\\  & =\\mu \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fact that $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$ is a desirable property. We'll meet so-called **biased estimators** in future lessons. \n\nFinally, any estimate $\\bar{x}$ of the population mean $\\mu$ found by computing the mean of a sample is called an **unbiased estimate** of $\\mu.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1065 6298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4c9ca398-f024-59cf-b484-80cf2f43cc8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158561 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean as an Unbiased Estimator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412692 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412692 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412692 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05b86b9d-3da6-5793-a9a2-ff8597d2d88f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412693 #edb/ref 1080 "2.25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412693 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412693 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a8f0bd0-23b0-55c2-ac0d-7b2cdb3481d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412694 #edb/ref 1080 "-3.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412694 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412694 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bad6d10-02bb-565e-9e3d-fc4d56f3515f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412695 #edb/ref 1080 "1.0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412695 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412695 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c9718c7-902f-5345-ad03-482984e9c1fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17